        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my breaker finder beep on every breaker?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Its sensitivity is too high for the panel. The signal couples onto neighbouring conductors, so the receiver hears it everywhere. Lower sensitivity in small steps, hold the receiver at a consistent angle, sweep in both directions, and replace the receiver battery if readings stay muddy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is my breaker finder not detecting anything?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually the transmitter is not sending a signal. Check its lights show the outlet is live, reset nearby GFCI outlets, and try another outlet on the same circuit. If it is live, unplug surge strips and electronics on the circuit that may be absorbing the signal."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do breaker finders work with AFCI breakers?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most do, but the electronics inside AFCI and GFCI breakers can make readings unreliable. A finder may point at the wrong AFCI breaker or miss the right one. Confirm any answer on those breakers by switching off and checking the outlet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can LED lights stop a breaker finder working?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They can. LED drivers, dimmers and smart switches can shunt the transmitter's signal before it reaches the panel. Turn off LED lighting and dimmers on the circuit, or test from an outlet rather than a light fixture."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is my breaker finder broken?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Rarely. Test it on a simple circuit with nothing else plugged in and a fresh battery. If it reliably finds that breaker, the tool is fine and the problem is the circuit or panel you were testing. If it fails there too, contact the manufacturer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does the finder work on some outlets but not others?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Outlets differ in what else shares their circuit and in how they are wired. A loose or back-stabbed receptacle, an open neutral, or a dimmer on the same circuit can each weaken the signal. Try another outlet on the circuit before blaming the finder."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What if no breaker turns the outlet off?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Check for a subpanel, and test each half of a split receptacle separately. If you still cannot de-energise it, stop. An outlet fed from somewhere unexpected is a wiring issue for a licensed electrician to trace safely."
          }
        }
